1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:03,820
Picture yourself signing the contract for a new Tesla Model Y.

2
00:00:04,040 --> 00:00:06,282
You picked the options, the color,

3
00:00:06,282 --> 00:00:08,789
and signed your name. In a few months,

4
00:00:08,789 --> 00:00:09,910
the car is yours.

5
00:00:10,130 --> 00:00:11,287
When that one car is sold,

6
00:00:11,287 --> 00:00:12,800
how much does Tesla actually keep?

7
00:00:13,620 --> 00:00:16,320
Short answer: under 2 million won.

8
00:00:16,540 --> 00:00:20,163
About 950 billion won of Q3 operating profit

9
00:00:20,163 --> 00:00:23,210
divided by roughly 490,000 cars sold.

10
00:00:23,430 --> 00:00:25,300
About the price of a new smartphone.

11
00:00:26,120 --> 00:00:29,177
A year ago it was about 4.3 million won per car.

12
00:00:29,177 --> 00:00:31,660
Now about 1.95 million, less than half.

13
00:00:31,880 --> 00:00:34,730
Let's follow what happened in between.

14
00:00:35,550 --> 00:00:39,170
Yet this company is worth about $1.5 trillion.

15
00:00:39,390 --> 00:00:41,307
Why would the market pay that for a carmaker

16
00:00:41,307 --> 00:00:43,050
that keeps a smartphone's worth per car?

17
00:00:43,270 --> 00:00:46,680
Let's find the answer by following Tesla's results.

18
00:00:47,500 --> 00:00:50,060
Stay to the end and you'll learn three things.

19
00:00:50,280 --> 00:00:53,540
One: the hidden money that first made Tesla profitable.

20
00:00:53,760 --> 00:00:55,399
Two: where the missing profit went,

21
00:00:55,399 --> 00:00:56,570
because it didn't vanish.

22
00:00:56,790 --> 00:01:00,389
Three: how much expectation today's price carries.

23
00:01:00,389 --> 00:01:04,060
Plus the companies Warren Buffett admits he missed.

24
00:01:04,880 --> 00:01:06,890
Let's rewind to 2020.

25
00:01:07,710 --> 00:01:10,380
Tesla's first full year in the black.

26
00:01:10,600 --> 00:01:12,552
Just two years earlier,

27
00:01:12,552 --> 00:01:15,778
it kept hearing it would soon go bust.

28
00:01:15,778 --> 00:01:17,560
So this was big news.

29
00:01:18,380 --> 00:01:20,930
But that year's results have one odd line.

30
00:01:21,150 --> 00:01:23,811
Money that didn't come from selling cars was

31
00:01:23,811 --> 00:01:26,170
more than double the year's net income.

32
00:01:26,390 --> 00:01:27,294
By simple math,

33
00:01:27,294 --> 00:01:30,430
without it Tesla might have posted a loss that year.

34
00:01:31,240 --> 00:01:33,491
It's called regulatory credits: points a

35
00:01:33,491 --> 00:01:35,911
carmaker earns by selling cars cleaner than

36
00:01:35,911 --> 00:01:37,880
government emissions rules require.

37
00:01:38,110 --> 00:01:39,020
Selling only EVs,

38
00:01:39,020 --> 00:01:40,787
Tesla always has points to spare,

39
00:01:40,787 --> 00:01:43,250
so it sells them to carmakers that fall short.

40
00:01:44,060 --> 00:01:48,316
The buyers are mostly big carmakers selling lots of gasoline cars.

41
00:01:48,316 --> 00:01:51,670
Buying Tesla's points was cheaper than paying fines.

42
00:01:52,490 --> 00:01:54,858
Making the points costs almost nothing,

43
00:01:54,858 --> 00:01:57,590
so it's like an allowance from the neighbors.

44
00:01:57,810 --> 00:02:00,950
That's the hidden money I mentioned first.

45
00:02:01,780 --> 00:02:04,587
What first held Tesla in the black wasn't only cars.

46
00:02:04,587 --> 00:02:05,720
It was these credits.

47
00:02:05,930 --> 00:02:06,753
Then last summer,

48
00:02:06,753 --> 00:02:09,850
other carmakers started losing their reason to buy these points.

49
00:02:10,670 --> 00:02:11,990
The culprit: a U.S. law.

50
00:02:12,810 --> 00:02:14,565
Signed on July 4 last year,

51
00:02:14,565 --> 00:02:16,450
Independence Day of all days,

52
00:02:16,450 --> 00:02:20,610
the law cut the fine for missing fuel-economy standards to zero.

53
00:02:20,830 --> 00:02:21,458
No fine,

54
00:02:21,458 --> 00:02:25,380
less reason for other carmakers to pay for points.

55
00:02:25,600 --> 00:02:26,321
And this year,

56
00:02:26,321 --> 00:02:29,720
Tesla's credit revenue shrank to about a quarter in a single year.

57
00:02:30,550 --> 00:02:32,857
Credit revenue of about 550 billion won a

58
00:02:32,857 --> 00:02:35,390
quarter a year ago fell to about 150 billion.

59
00:02:35,610 --> 00:02:38,500
A pay cut like that would make anyone sigh.

60
00:02:39,320 --> 00:02:41,851
It briefly rose right after the law changed,

61
00:02:41,851 --> 00:02:43,289
then fell fast this year.

62
00:02:43,289 --> 00:02:46,510
Fewer points bought by others is seen as one big reason.

63
00:02:47,320 --> 00:02:49,054
So is Tesla in trouble?

64
00:02:49,054 --> 00:02:51,090
Here comes the first twist.

65
00:02:51,920 --> 00:02:54,700
Set the credits aside and look at the core business: cars.

66
00:02:55,520 --> 00:02:56,276
Surprisingly,

67
00:02:56,276 --> 00:02:58,950
building and selling cars actually got better.

68
00:02:59,170 --> 00:03:00,985
Of every 100 won in car sales,

69
00:03:00,985 --> 00:03:03,828
what's left after production costs rose from 15

70
00:03:03,828 --> 00:03:05,340
won a year ago to 18 won.

71
00:03:06,160 --> 00:03:08,657
Yet the whole company's operating profit fell

72
00:03:08,657 --> 00:03:10,210
by more than half in a year.

73
00:03:10,420 --> 00:03:12,801
Split the drop into thirds and the lost

74
00:03:12,801 --> 00:03:14,511
allowance explains only one.

75
00:03:14,511 --> 00:03:16,160
Where did the other two go?

76
00:03:16,980 --> 00:03:18,766
The second culprit, surprisingly,

77
00:03:18,766 --> 00:03:19,632
is Tesla itself.

78
00:03:19,632 --> 00:03:22,230
It grew its spending far faster than its income.

79
00:03:22,450 --> 00:03:24,581
The spending it raised itself was bigger than

80
00:03:24,581 --> 00:03:25,670
the credits others cut.

81
00:03:26,490 --> 00:03:29,840
Promise number two: where the profit went.

82
00:03:30,660 --> 00:03:33,615
Revenue is about the same as a year ago,

83
00:03:33,615 --> 00:03:35,609
but R&D went up nearly 70%.

84
00:03:35,609 --> 00:03:38,933
Like a family with a flat salary that sharply

85
00:03:38,933 --> 00:03:40,780
raised the kids' tuition.

86
00:03:40,990 --> 00:03:44,263
Spending on new factories and equipment more than doubled.

87
00:03:44,263 --> 00:03:45,900
That hits cash before profit.

88
00:03:46,120 --> 00:03:49,235
The R&D increase is bigger than the profit drop.

89
00:03:49,235 --> 00:03:52,610
Even the extra money from cars went into the future.

90
00:03:53,430 --> 00:03:56,430
Tesla says most of the extra R&D goes to AI for

91
00:03:56,430 --> 00:03:57,962
self-driving and robots,

92
00:03:57,962 --> 00:04:00,260
racing toward what comes after cars.

93
00:04:01,080 --> 00:04:01,761
Per car,

94
00:04:01,761 --> 00:04:05,592
Tesla keeps about 1.95 million won but spends

95
00:04:05,592 --> 00:04:07,720
about 7.5 million on R&D.

96
00:04:07,940 --> 00:04:09,764
Nearly four times what it keeps,

97
00:04:09,764 --> 00:04:10,790
bet on the future.

98
00:04:11,600 --> 00:04:15,480
So cash left after investment is less than half of last year's.

99
00:04:15,710 --> 00:04:19,920
Luckily it holds about 60 trillion won in cash and investments,

100
00:04:19,920 --> 00:04:22,860
so it isn't in danger. A big emergency fund.

101
00:04:23,080 --> 00:04:25,640
So where exactly is all this money being bet?

102
00:04:26,470 --> 00:04:30,070
Three businesses meant to bring in money after the car is sold.

103
00:04:30,890 --> 00:04:31,668
One: FSD,

104
00:04:31,668 --> 00:04:35,560
software literally named 'Full Self-Driving'.

105
00:04:35,780 --> 00:04:38,220
Set a destination and the car changes lanes,

106
00:04:38,220 --> 00:04:40,271
stops at lights and turns on its own.

107
00:04:40,271 --> 00:04:42,656
But the driver still has to watch the road,

108
00:04:42,656 --> 00:04:44,430
so it's still driver assistance.

109
00:04:45,240 --> 00:04:49,670
You buy it once with the car or pay a monthly subscription.

110
00:04:50,490 --> 00:04:56,250
Paying FSD users rose from 1.04 million to 1.74 million in a year.

111
00:04:56,470 --> 00:04:59,207
Tesla says more than three in four new buyers

112
00:04:59,207 --> 00:05:00,910
in North America pay for it.

113
00:05:01,130 --> 00:05:04,510
Money that keeps coming every month after the sale.

114
00:05:05,330 --> 00:05:09,130
Two: the Cybercab, a two-seat robotaxi.

115
00:05:09,950 --> 00:05:11,960
Inside, no steering wheel or pedals.

116
00:05:11,960 --> 00:05:14,640
It began carrying riders in Austin in September.

117
00:05:15,460 --> 00:05:16,520
With no driver,

118
00:05:16,520 --> 00:05:19,418
one car could carry riders day and night.

119
00:05:19,418 --> 00:05:21,044
That's Tesla's picture,

120
00:05:21,044 --> 00:05:23,800
though it's still starting in one city.

121
00:05:24,610 --> 00:05:25,698
Three: Optimus,

122
00:05:25,698 --> 00:05:28,745
a humanoid robot meant to take over simple

123
00:05:28,745 --> 00:05:32,300
repetitive work like carrying parts in factories.

124
00:05:32,530 --> 00:05:34,540
First deliveries to outside customers are

125
00:05:34,540 --> 00:05:36,010
targeted for around late 2027.

126
00:05:36,830 --> 00:05:39,081
The hands are still the hard part.

127
00:05:39,081 --> 00:05:42,060
Robots remind us how amazing human hands are.

128
00:05:42,880 --> 00:05:44,116
Beyond the three bets,

129
00:05:44,116 --> 00:05:46,250
one business already pulls its weight.

130
00:05:46,480 --> 00:05:49,654
Megapack: a container-sized battery that stores

131
00:05:49,654 --> 00:05:52,760
power made during the day for use when needed.

132
00:05:53,580 --> 00:05:56,868
It keeps about 33 won of every 100 won sold,

133
00:05:56,868 --> 00:05:58,587
nearly double cars' 18.

134
00:05:58,587 --> 00:06:01,950
A real blessing for a company with heavy R&D.

135
00:06:02,770 --> 00:06:05,299
So how big must these three bets get for

136
00:06:05,299 --> 00:06:07,070
today's price to make sense?

137
00:06:07,890 --> 00:06:10,338
Now let's borrow the method of Benjamin Graham,

138
00:06:10,338 --> 00:06:11,640
Warren Buffett's teacher.

139
00:06:12,460 --> 00:06:14,963
Graham's method has three steps. First,

140
00:06:14,963 --> 00:06:17,595
take average earnings over several years,

141
00:06:17,595 --> 00:06:18,750
not one good year.

142
00:06:18,970 --> 00:06:22,617
Then pay at most ten times that average,

143
00:06:22,617 --> 00:06:25,990
a price you'd earn back in ten years.

144
00:06:26,210 --> 00:06:31,280
And count future growth as zero. A strict floor.

145
00:06:31,510 --> 00:06:35,953
Tesla's average earnings per share are about $0.97.

146
00:06:35,953 --> 00:06:38,740
Times ten gives $9.72 per share.

147
00:06:39,560 --> 00:06:44,759
But at the Oct 23 close Tesla traded at $372.2,

148
00:06:44,759 --> 00:06:46,640
38.3x that value.

149
00:06:46,870 --> 00:06:50,110
Yes, I thought my calculator was broken too.

150
00:06:50,320 --> 00:06:51,913
By the same yardstick,

151
00:06:51,913 --> 00:06:54,591
Tesla must earn dozens of times more.

152
00:06:54,591 --> 00:06:58,500
The market is betting the three bets succeed that big.

153
00:06:59,320 --> 00:07:03,276
ValueNote runs this same calculator on over 600

154
00:07:03,276 --> 00:07:04,960
companies every day.

155
00:07:05,180 --> 00:07:07,584
But it's a floor that counts dreams as zero,

156
00:07:07,584 --> 00:07:10,370
so it's not a target price or a buy or sell signal.

157
00:07:11,190 --> 00:07:13,663
But this strict calculator once made Warren

158
00:07:13,663 --> 00:07:15,100
Buffett himself miss big.

159
00:07:15,920 --> 00:07:17,432
At Berkshire Hathaway's 2017 annual meeting,

160
00:07:17,432 --> 00:07:18,944
Warren Buffett said this about Amazon: I was

161
00:07:18,944 --> 00:07:20,490
too dumb to realize what was going to happen.

162
00:07:20,710 --> 00:07:22,910
On Google, he admitted he blew it.

163
00:07:23,720 --> 00:07:25,828
Amazon poured nearly everything it earned back

164
00:07:25,828 --> 00:07:27,112
into warehouses and servers,

165
00:07:27,112 --> 00:07:29,220
so on profit alone it always looked expensive.

166
00:07:29,440 --> 00:07:31,616
Companies that spend heavily on the future

167
00:07:31,616 --> 00:07:33,689
always look expensive on today's profit.

168
00:07:33,689 --> 00:07:35,710
Whether Tesla is one, nobody knows yet.

169
00:07:36,530 --> 00:07:38,844
If robotaxis and subscriptions become big money,

170
00:07:38,844 --> 00:07:41,880
today's spending is seed money and the strict math was too low.

171
00:07:42,100 --> 00:07:43,972
If commercialization slips,

172
00:07:43,972 --> 00:07:46,814
Tesla waits with credits down and R&D up.

173
00:07:46,814 --> 00:07:50,350
Timelines like these often run longer than planned.

174
00:07:51,170 --> 00:07:52,968
So this isn't buy or sell.

175
00:07:52,968 --> 00:07:56,770
It's about seeing which future the price is betting on.

176
00:07:57,590 --> 00:08:00,433
At the next earnings, watch three things.

177
00:08:00,433 --> 00:08:02,929
One: can cars stand without credits?

178
00:08:02,929 --> 00:08:05,980
Watch whether 18 won per 100 drops below 15.

179
00:08:06,200 --> 00:08:09,370
Two: is it dipping into its cash pile? In Q2,

180
00:08:09,370 --> 00:08:12,470
cash after investment was actually negative.

181
00:08:12,690 --> 00:08:15,797
Three: are the three bets starting to earn?

182
00:08:15,797 --> 00:08:18,470
Paying FSD users and robotaxi cities.

183
00:08:19,280 --> 00:08:23,062
Curious which of the three moves first at the next earnings?

184
00:08:23,062 --> 00:08:25,710
Subscribe, like and turn on notifications.

185
00:08:25,930 --> 00:08:27,751
Watch our SpaceX episode,

186
00:08:27,751 --> 00:08:29,645
Elon Musk's other company,

187
00:08:29,645 --> 00:08:31,320
for the bigger picture.

188
00:08:31,540 --> 00:08:33,174
Seed money or a burden?

189
00:08:33,174 --> 00:08:35,377
Place your bet in the comments.

190
00:08:35,377 --> 00:08:38,859
ValueNote is here to help you invest more wisely.

191
00:08:38,859 --> 00:08:39,570
Thank you.
